          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do European and US bodies differ on when to start medication for stage 1 hypertension?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** Yes, the two regions use different blood-pressure labels, which changes who counts as having stage 1 disease. The sources I could access do not give a clear side-by-side comparison of when each body starts medication, and they do not report how strong the evidence is for either position.\n\n**Where they differ (diagnostic labels):** One guidance document says the 130/80 mm Hg threshold is a US position. European guidance starts office hypertension at 140/90 mm Hg and treats readings of 130 to 139 / 80 to 89 as high-normal, not stage 1 (*Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*). Because of this, a large group that US guidance calls stage 1 falls into the European high-normal band. That same document says any answer quoting a threshold should state which convention it uses.\n\n**Where the sources describe a shared approach:** A consensus summary (*Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line*) ties the decision to cardiovascular risk rather than the number alone. It says:\n- People with established cardiovascular disease, diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or an estimated 10-year cardiovascular risk of 10% or more with stage 1 hypertension should start drug treatment alongside lifestyle changes.\n- People with stage 1 hypertension without those features should try lifestyle changes first and be reassessed after three to six months before medication is considered.\n\nThis summary does not name which body issued that rule, so I cannot say it is the European or the US position.\n\n**Where the sources say guidance disagrees:** The same summary says bodies differ on how aggressively to treat low-risk stage 1 hypertension. It links this to the threshold difference above, so many people the US calls stage 1 are advised on lifestyle alone under European labels.\n\n**What I could not find:** The sources do not include the specific recommendation wording and strength grades from each body, or the trial evidence behind them. So I cannot tell you how firm either position is or which trials support it. For that, the original ESC/ESH and ACC/AHA guideline documents would need to be checked directly, and a clinician can help apply them to a particular person's risk.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
